export default "Cervical mucus is produced by crypts (small pockets) in the cervical canal, and its properties are directly controlled by estrogen and progesterone. Understanding what each type signals transforms a commonly misunderstood bodily fluid into a reliable fertility sign.\n\n## The Complete Cycle Pattern\n\n### Days 1-5: Menstruation\n\nDuring menstruation, the uterine lining and blood obscure any cervical mucus observation. Most people defer mucus checking until after bleeding has stopped.\n\n### Immediately After Menstruation: Dry Phase\n\nAfter menstrual bleeding ends, estrogen is at its lowest point. Many people experience 2-4 days of dryness, no visible mucus at the vaginal opening. In the fertility awareness framework, this is a relatively infertile time (though not entirely, particularly for people with shorter cycles where ovulation could come earlier).\n\n**What you observe:** Nothing (just the normal sensation of skin against skin), or tiny amounts of opaque white discharge.\n\n**What it means hormonally:** Estrogen is at its monthly nadir; the cervix is producing minimal mucus.\n\n---\n\n### Early Follicular Phase: Sticky Type\n\nAs follicles begin developing under FSH stimulation, estrogen starts rising. The cervix begins producing small amounts of mucus, but it is still dense and non-fertile.\n\n**What you observe:**\n- Small amounts of white, pale yellow, or beige mucus\n- Pasty or crumbly texture; it breaks when stretched and does not string out\n- Tacky; sticks to skin and toilet paper\n- No lubrication sensation\n\n**What it means:** Estrogen is rising but not yet at levels to produce fertile mucus. Sperm cannot penetrate this mucus type effectively.\n\n---\n\n### Mid-Follicular Phase: Creamy Type\n\nEstrogen continues rising. Mucus volume increases and texture shifts toward more fluid.\n\n**What you observe:**\n- White to slightly yellow, or opaque\n- Lotion-like, smooth, slightly viscous\n- Some stretch (1-2cm before breaking)\n- Mild lubrication sensation\n\n**What it means:** Estrogen is climbing; fertility is increasing. This type is transitional, more fertile than sticky, less fertile than egg-white. The Billings method marks this as the beginning of the fertile window.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-Ovulatory Phase: Egg-White (Peak Fertile)\n\nAt high estrogen, typically 1-3 days before ovulation, cervical mucus reaches its most fertile form. This is the most important type to recognize.\n\n**What you observe:**\n- Clear to slightly cloudy or white\n- Extremely slippery and lubricating; you may feel it as dampness or lubrication when moving\n- Stretches 2-5cm or more between fingers without breaking\n- May be abundant, noticeable on underwear, visible when wiping\n- Resembles raw egg whites (hence the name)\n\n**The stretch test:** Collect a small sample on your fingertip. Touch your thumb to it. Slowly move them apart. If it stretches more than 2-3cm without breaking, it is egg-white quality.\n\n**What it means:** Estrogen is at or near its cycle peak. Ovulation will occur within 1-3 days. This is the most fertile time of the cycle. Sperm can survive 3-5 days in this mucus type (vs. hours in sticky or dry conditions).\n\n**Peak day:** The last day you observe this type of mucus is the \"peak day,\" typically the day of or the day before ovulation. It is identified retrospectively: when today's mucus is sticky or dry, yesterday was peak day.\n\n---\n\n### Post-Ovulation: Return to Dry or Sticky\n\nAfter ovulation, the corpus luteum produces progesterone. Progesterone does the opposite of estrogen: it causes the cervix to produce thick, sticky mucus that forms a plug, blocking sperm entry.\n\n**What you observe:**\n- Rapid transition back to sticky or dry\n- Sometimes a brief appearance of white, opaque mucus before drying\n- The slippery quality disappears abruptly, often within 24 hours of ovulation\n\nThis abrupt shift is one of the most reliable signs of ovulation having occurred. In the Billings method, the 3rd day of consistently non-fertile mucus after peak day marks the beginning of the infertile post-ovulatory phase.\n\n---\n\n### Late Luteal Phase: Variable\n\nIn the late luteal phase, mucus typically remains dry or minimally sticky. Some people notice a slight increase in discharge as the cervix prepares for menstruation, but this is usually creamy-white and non-fertile.\n\n---\n\n## Logging Mucus: The Daily Practice\n\nEffective mucus observation requires consistency. The Billings Ovulation Method uses a daily sensation and observation system:\n\n**Sensation check:** Throughout the day, note whether you feel dry, moist, or slippery at the vaginal opening. This is more sensitive than single daily checks.\n\n**Observation check:** At least once daily, observe mucus directly using toilet paper or fingertips.\n\n**Standard notation:**\n\n| Code | Type | Description |\n|------|------|-------------|\n| D | Dry | Nothing visible, not moist |\n| S | Sticky | White/yellow, doesn't stretch |\n| C | Creamy | Lotion-like, slight stretch |\n| EW | Egg-white | Clear, slippery, stretches 2-5cm+ |\n| P | Peak day | Last day of EW (identified retrospectively) |\n\n---\n\n## What Abnormal Mucus Patterns Suggest\n\n**No egg-white mucus:** Low estrogen peak (anovulatory cycle, hypothalamic amenorrhea, low-dose hormonal contraception, antihistamine use), or dehydration.\n\n**Consistently dry:** Possible low estrogen, hormonal medication, or significant dehydration.\n\n**Persistent fertile-type mucus:** In PCOS, multiple estrogen surges without ovulation can produce multiple patches of fertile mucus, making pattern interpretation more complex.\n\n**Sudden change in normal pattern:** New medications, significant stress, illness, or thyroid changes can all alter mucus patterns.\n\n---\n\n## Distinguishing from Infection\n\nThis is important to know and easy to confuse:\n\n**Normal fertile mucus:**\n- Clear to slightly opaque\n- Slippery and stretchy\n- Odorless or very mild scent\n- No discomfort\n\n**Bacterial vaginosis (BV):**\n- Thin, gray-white, homogeneous\n- Watery\n- Distinctly fishy or ammoniac odor, especially after sex\n- May have mild irritation but often asymptomatic\n\n**Vulvovaginal candidiasis (yeast infection):**\n- Thick, white, chunky or cottage-cheese texture\n- Does NOT stretch\n- Odorless or mild yeasty odor\n- Itching, burning, and vulvar irritation\n\n**Trichomoniasis:**\n- Yellow-green, frothy, bubbly\n- Foul odor\n- Itching, burning, redness\n\nIf you notice odor, itching, or an unusual texture that does not match the cycle-phase pattern, this is not fertile mucus. It warrants evaluation.\n\n---\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nDaily cervical mucus logging in Floriva creates the pattern data that makes the fertility window visible. Over 2-3 cycles of consistent observation, you will identify your typical mucus pattern, know roughly how many egg-white days you have before ovulation, and notice when the pattern deviates, which is clinically meaningful information about your hormonal health.";
